UID:tech-policy-press-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T222051Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260924
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260925
SUMMARY:Deadline: Tech Policy Press Fellowship Program 2027
DESCRIPTION:Tech Policy Press is recruiting its 2027 fellowship cohort to p
 ursue independent reporting and analysis on urgent questions at the inters
 ection of technology and democracy. Since 2023 the programme has supported
  25 fellows from 11 countries across three cohorts\, writing on AI governa
 nce\, tech regulation\, digital rights and the entanglement of state and c
 orporate power. WHO IT IS FOR: mid-career journalists\, researchers\, lawy
 ers and public policy professionals with a strong background in a specific
  tech policy area\, a particular geography\, or technology's impact on dem
 ocracy and society. Applicants from the Global Majority and from communiti
 es underrepresented in media are encouraged\, as are projects that centre 
 communities and regions usually excluded from global tech policy conversat
 ions. Contributions can range from original reporting to policy analysis\,
  and technical practitioners are welcome as long as the work fits the publ
 ication's editorial purview. EXPECTATIONS: at least one piece of analysis 
 or reporting per month (exclusive to Tech Policy Press\, though it may be 
 republished elsewhere)\, a mandatory hour-long monthly cohort meeting\, a 
 monthly check-in with the editorial team\, collaboration with other fellow
 s and the masthead\, agreed deadlines\, and disclosure of conflicts of int
 erest. Applicants should arrive with a clear area of focus and a sense of 
 how the work will inform both the public and decision-makers. APPLY via th
 e online form by 11:59pm EDT on 24 September 2026\, with a CV or resume\, 
 a cover letter of no more than two pages\, links to prior publications (op
 en access\, published within the past three years\, at least one in Englis
 h and at least one solo-authored\; social posts\, pre-prints and unpublish
 ed coursework do not count) and two professional references. Shortlisted c
 andidates may be invited to an online interview. Decisions are expected by
  early December 2026. Two optional Zoom information sessions run on 9 Sept
 ember at 8am EDT and 16 September at 7pm EDT\; registration required.\n\nO
 rganisation: Tech Policy Press\nLocation: Fully remote\, worldwide. Intern
 ational applicants explicitly encouraged\; fellows work as independent con
 tractors. No requirement to be authorised to work in the USA.\nAward: USD 
 10\,000 stipend per fellow\, paid quarterly. Also included: editorial guid
 ance from the Tech Policy Press team\, membership of an international coho
 rt with regular feedback and exchange\, and monthly sessions to strengthen
  the work. Fellows' work is published on the Tech Policy Press website and
  promoted through the newsletter\, podcast and events. No application fee.
 \nDuration: Twelve months\, part-time\, running January to December 2027\n
 \nMore: https://www.techpolicy.press/call-for-applications-2027-tech-polic
 y-press-fellowship-program/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medi
 um=referral
URL:https://www.techpolicy.press/call-for-applications-2027-tech-policy-pre
 ss-fellowship-program/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=re
 ferral
LOCATION:Fully remote\, worldwide. International applicants explicitly enco
 uraged\; fellows work as independent contractors. No requirement to be aut
 horised to work in the USA.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:sundance-episodic-program-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T222051Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260825
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260826
SUMMARY:Deadline: Sundance Institute: Episodic Program and Lab 2027
DESCRIPTION:The Sundance Institute Episodic Program is an entry point for e
 merging to mid-career writers and creators working across all scripted ser
 ies platforms\, combining a lab experience with customised support to move
  projects and careers forward. The Episodic Lab lets writers workshop an o
 riginal pilot and develop their writing and pitching skills: working with 
 showrunners\, producers and executives\, fellows take part in one-on-one s
 tory meetings\, pitching sessions\, craft workshops and writers rooms. Up 
 to ten applicants are selected\, either for the in-person Lab (preceded by
  virtual workshops) or for a fully virtual Intensive with a creative advis
 or. ELIGIBILITY: applicants must be 18 or older\; the script must be the a
 pplicant's original creation or adapted from optioned source material and 
 written in English\; the draft must be revised or new\, not already produc
 ed\, not sold to a third party and not distributed online\; and applicants
  cannot have previously had an episodic project (pilot or pitch) produced 
 by a studio or network. Open to international and U.S. participants\, subj
 ect to travel guidelines. Up to two writers per project may participate\, 
 with one named as primary applicant. APPLICATION: submitted via FilmFreewa
 y\, including writer bios (250 words each)\, a series logline (50 words)\,
  pilot synopsis (500 words)\, one-page series overview (500 words)\, perso
 nal statement (250 words)\, community connection\, thematic statement (250
  words)\, characters and core relationships (250 words)\, episodic vision\
 , optional video samples (up to three links\, 10 minutes total)\, and the 
 full pilot script as a PDF in standard screenplay format. Demographic ques
 tions are optional and do not affect consideration. Candidates may submit 
 up to three applications\, with a separate application and USD 50 fee for 
 each project. Submissions opened 13 July 2026 and close 25 August 2026 at 
 6:00 p.m. PT. Finalists are notified by email through FilmFreeway around m
 id to late March 2027 (FilmFreeway lists 31 March 2027)\, and the list of 
 selected writers and projects is published at sundance.org/epi after 15 Ap
 ril 2027. Finalists must return a Fellow Agreement\, a Participation Agree
 ment and any other required documents within five days. Questions: episodi
 cprogram@sundance.org.\n\nOrganisation: Sundance Institute\nLocation: Open
  to international and U.S. applicants. In-person Lab takes place in the At
 lanta\, Georgia area\, USA in Spring 2027\; the Intensive track is fully o
 nline.\nAward: No stipend or honorarium for participation. For the in-pers
 on Lab\, Sundance covers round-trip basic economy airfare from the airport
  nearest the fellow's residence\, shared housing with a private room and b
 ath\, and ground transport to and from the airport (fellows within 100 mil
 es of the Lab get ground transport only). Meals\, in-room charges\, travel
  insurance\, luggage fees and taxes are the fellow's own cost. One selecte
 d project also receives a separate monetary grant from the Alfred P. Sloan
  Foundation for a series with an illuminating emphasis on science\, includ
 ing medicine\, energy\, technology or invention. APPLICATION FEE: USD 50 n
 onrefundable processing fee per project submitted.\nDuration: In-person La
 b in Spring 2027 (event dates 15 to 20 April 2027) preceded by virtual wor
 kshops\; the Intensive track convenes virtually in Summer 2027.\n\nMore: h
 ttps://filmfreeway.com/SundanceEpisodicLab2027?utm_source=artificialnouvea
 ugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://filmfreeway.com/SundanceEpisodicLab2027?utm_source=artificialno
 uveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Open to international and U.S. applicants. In-person Lab takes pla
 ce in the Atlanta\, Georgia area\, USA in Spring 2027\; the Intensive trac
 k is fully online.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:beinecke-short-term-fellowships-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T222051Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260815
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260816
SUMMARY:Deadline: Beinecke Library Short-Term Research Fellowships 2027-202
 8 (Yale)
DESCRIPTION:The Beinecke Library Fellowship Program offers competitive shor
 t-term fellowships to support research projects that substantively engage 
 with Yale Library Special Collections materials. WHO CAN APPLY: academic a
 nd independent scholars\, locally and globally\, with or without an instit
 utional affiliation\; the library explicitly welcomes applicants regardles
 s of institutional association\, race\, cultural background\, ability\, se
 xual orientation\, gender or socioeconomic status. Applications using trad
 itional archival and bibliographic research methods are encouraged\, and s
 o are creative\, interdisciplinary and non-traditional approaches to worki
 ng with the collections\, which makes this a live option for artists and w
 riters whose practice is research-driven. WHAT YOU GET: USD 5\,000 per mon
 th for an onsite research visit of between two weeks and two months\, paid
  at the start of the fellowship whenever possible (international fellows s
 hould expect payment to take several weeks)\; fellows pay their own taxes 
 on the award. TIMELINE: applications for the 2027-2028 awards opened 1 Jun
 e 2026 and close 15 August 2026\, via Interfolio\; all decisions are notif
 ied by 31 January 2027\; research must be conducted between 1 July 2027 an
 d 30 June 2028\, and awards cannot be deferred.\n\nOrganisation: Beinecke 
 Rare Book & Manuscript Library\, Yale University\nLocation: Onsite at Yale
  Library Special Collections\, New Haven\, Connecticut\, USA. Open to acad
 emic and independent scholars locally and globally\, regardless of institu
 tional association.\nAward: USD 5\,000 per month for research visits betwe
 en two weeks and two months. Funding is paid at the beginning of the fello
 wship where possible (payment to international fellows often takes several
  weeks)\; fellows are responsible for taxes on the award. Free to apply.\n
 Duration: Two weeks to two months of onsite research\, conducted between 1
  July 2027 and 30 June 2028\; awards cannot be deferred\n\nMore: https://b
 einecke.library.yale.edu/beinecke/researchers/fellowships/short-term?utm_s
 ource=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://beinecke.library.yale.edu/beinecke/researchers/fellowships/shor
 t-term?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Onsite at Yale Library Special Collections\, New Haven\, Connectic
 ut\, USA. Open to academic and independent scholars locally and globally\,
  regardless of institutional association.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:extra-teeth-submissions-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T222051Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261214
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261215
SUMMARY:Deadline: Extra Teeth: Open Submissions for Fiction and Nonfiction 
 (paid)
DESCRIPTION:Extra Teeth is a Scottish literary magazine with an internation
 al outlook\, paying for fiction and nonfiction. Submissions are currently 
 open. No theme is set per issue\; send your best work on any topic. Pieces
  should be 800 to 4\,000 words\, previously unpublished\, submitted as a W
 ord document (standard font\, size 12\, double line spacing) titled with '
 F' for fiction or 'NF' for nonfiction followed by your name\, plus a title
  sheet (name\, address\, word count\, bio under 100 words)\; cover letters
  are not required. Send one piece only per submission period to submission
 s@extrateeth.co.uk with 'SHORT STORY: TITLE' or 'ESSAY: TITLE' in the subj
 ect line\; secondary submissions are deleted. Copyright remains with the a
 uthor. Work in translation is accepted with the necessary permissions. The
  magazine does not currently publish poetry\, scripts or artwork (aside fr
 om its guest illustrator). PAYMENT: GBP 200 per piece for the magazine plu
 s two contributor copies\; four pieces are also chosen for the With Bite S
 ubstack at GBP 150 each. AI POLICY: no AI-written work is published or con
 sidered. ILLUSTRATORS: one guest illustrator is commissioned per issue (pa
 id GBP 943.90)\; send work to hello@extrateeth.co.uk with 'ILLUSTRATOR' in
  the subject line\; marginalised illustrators especially encouraged. Allow
  eight weeks after the submission period before querying. Check the submis
 sions page for current open periods.\n\nOrganisation: Extra Teeth\nLocatio
 n: Scottish magazine with an international outlook. Open to writers anywhe
 re\; Scottish writers especially championed\, and submissions from underre
 presented groups especially welcome.\nAward: GBP 200 per piece selected fo
 r the magazine\, plus two copies of the issue. Four pieces are also select
 ed for the With Bite Substack at GBP 150 each. Guest illustrator (one per 
 issue) is paid GBP 943.90. Paid by bank transfer within 30 days of a signe
 d publishing agreement. No submission fee.\nDuration: Per-issue publicatio
 n\; submissions accepted during open submission periods.\n\nMore: https://
 www.extrateeth.co.uk/submissions?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm
 _medium=referral
URL:https://www.extrateeth.co.uk/submissions?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Scottish magazine with an international outlook. Open to writers a
 nywhere\; Scottish writers especially championed\, and submissions from un
 derrepresented groups especially welcome.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:pulitzer-center-ai-accountability-fellowships-2026-2027@artificialnouve
 au.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T222051Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260712
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260713
SUMMARY:Deadline: Pulitzer Center: AI Accountability Fellowships 2026-2027
DESCRIPTION:The AI Accountability Fellowships support journalists working o
 n in-depth AI accountability stories that examine how governments and corp
 orations use predictive\, generative and surveillance technologies to guid
 e decisions in policing\, medicine\, social welfare\, criminal justice\, h
 iring and more. Designed for reporters from all beats\, desks and formats\
 , the fellowship asks applicants to propose a concrete reporting project (
 with evidence of pre-reporting) that uses approaches such as data analysis
 \, records requests and shoe-leather reporting to examine the real-world i
 mpact of algorithms. For the first time this year\, the fellowship also in
 cludes funding\, mentorship and training to develop and execute an impact/
 engagement plan to reach strategic audiences. SUPPORT: up to USD 25\,000 p
 er fellow (up to USD 20\,000 reporting plus USD 5\,000 engagement)\, paid 
 in three installments\; freelancers may budget up to one third as a stipen
 d\, while newsrooms cover staff salaries. Fellows also receive mentors\, s
 pecialized training\, pro bono legal and public records support\, and a la
 sting community of peers. ELIGIBILITY: staff or freelance journalists acro
 ss print\, radio\, video and multimedia\, able to work collaboratively\; r
 eporters may be based anywhere (fellowship is remote). Experience reportin
 g on AI is not required\, but a track record of in-depth\, impactful repor
 ting and investigative/data/explanatory experience is valued. Small teams 
 may apply with a designated lead Fellow. REQUIREMENTS: a mandatory monthly
  1.5-2 hour meeting\, at least one community call\, engagement with other 
 fellows\, and sharing of methodologies and lessons learned\; fellowship co
 mmunication\, meetings and training are in English\, but fellows may publi
 sh in any language. APPLY: submit a 500-word statement of purpose\, a 500-
 word project description with reporting plan\, an engagement plan\, a budg
 et\, three links to recent work\, a letter of commitment from a publishing
  media organization (or editor support letter for staff)\, three reference
 s and a CV. Funded with support from the MacArthur Foundation\, Luminate\,
  Omidyar Network and others. Contact: reacheditorial@pulitzercenter.org. D
 eadline: 12 July 2026\, 11:59pm EDT.\n\nOrganisation: Pulitzer Center\nLoc
 ation: Worldwide\; reporters may be based anywhere and the fellowship is r
 emote. Proposals from the Global South and from journalists representing a
  broad array of backgrounds are encouraged.\nAward: Up to USD 25\,000 per 
 fellow\, paid in three installments (up to USD 20\,000 for reporting and U
 SD 5\,000 for engagement activities)\; covers records requests\, travel\, 
 data analysis and engagement costs. Freelancers may budget up to one third
  of the total as a stipend\; newsrooms are expected to pay staff salaries.
  No application fee.\nDuration: 10 months\, expected September 2026 to Jul
 y 2027\n\nMore: https://pulitzercenter.org/grants-fellowships/opportunitie
 s-journalists/ai-accountability-fellowships?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://pulitzercenter.org/grants-fellowships/opportunities-journalists
 /ai-accountability-fellowships?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_m
 edium=referral
LOCATION:Worldwide\; reporters may be based anywhere and the fellowship is 
 remote. Proposals from the Global South and from journalists representing 
 a broad array of backgrounds are encouraged.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:iwmf-kari-howard-fund-narrative-journalism-2026@artificialnouveau.githu
 b.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T222051Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260712
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260713
SUMMARY:Deadline: IWMF: The Kari Howard Fund for Narrative Journalism 2026
DESCRIPTION:Established in memory of editor Kari Howard\, who championed na
 rrative journalism that wove the music of everyday life into stories illum
 inating the most important issues of the day. Offered by Kari's family and
  friends together with the International Women's Media Foundation\, the fu
 nd supports narrative journalism projects. Grants average around USD 5\,00
 0 (based on previous years). ELIGIBILITY: open to women and nonbinary jour
 nalists. Applicants may be a print journalist or a print journalist leadin
 g a multi-media team\, and may be freelance or staff\; they may apply indi
 vidually or as part of a multi-format team. Professional journalism must b
 e the applicant's primary profession\, with three or more years of profess
 ional experience (internships do not count). Applicants must show proof of
  interest from an editor or a proven track record of publication in promin
 ent media outlets. Applicants must apply and publish in English\; projects
  published anywhere in the world are eligible\, and stories may be publish
 ed digitally (not necessarily in a newspaper or magazine). Any multi-forma
 t reporting must supplement a printed project. All reporting and publishin
 g must be completed within six months of the award. Applicants receive a d
 ecision by early September. DEADLINE: 12 July 2026\, 11:59pm EST.\n\nOrgan
 isation: International Women's Media Foundation (IWMF)\nLocation: Worldwid
 e\; projects reported and published in English anywhere in the world. Open
  to women and nonbinary journalists.\nAward: Grants average around USD 5\,
 000 (based on previous years). No application fee.\nDuration: All reportin
 g and publishing must be completed within six months of the award\n\nMore:
  https://iwmf.submittable.com/submit/c65a15f2-9ff5-4c2b-b2a3-2d897c703381/
 the-kari-howard-fund-for-narrative-journalism-2026?utm_source=artificialno
 uveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://iwmf.submittable.com/submit/c65a15f2-9ff5-4c2b-b2a3-2d897c70338
 1/the-kari-howard-fund-for-narrative-journalism-2026?utm_source=artificial
 nouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Worldwide\; projects reported and published in English anywhere in
  the world. Open to women and nonbinary journalists.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:tractor-beam-water-issue-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T222051Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260630
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260701
SUMMARY:Deadline: Tractor Beam: Issue 6 'The Water Issue' - Call for Submis
 sions
DESCRIPTION:Tractor Beam's sixth issue\, themed around water in soil\, grow
 th\, land and ecosystems large and small\, is open for submissions. Editor
 s are seeking anti-apocalyptic visions that explore the future of water in
  farming and food production\, island ecologies\, hybrid sea-soil technolo
 gies\, the people who move water and the people water moves\, plus stories
  about drought\, diaspora and what gets carried downstream. FORMAT: storie
 s under 6\,000 words\; comics 12-16 panels. Submissions accepted via the c
 all page on Tractor Beam's Substack.\n\nOrganisation: Tractor Beam\nLocati
 on: Online publication (remote submissions\; geographic eligibility not st
 ated)\nAward: $1\,000 USD per accepted submission. Accepted work is consid
 ered for publication online (Tractor Beam website and Substack) and\, in s
 ome cases\, for one-off printed editions. Stories may also be considered f
 or the Protopian Prize with contributor consent. No submission fee mention
 ed.\nDuration: One-off published piece (no residency component)\n\nMore: h
 ttps://tractorbeamearth.substack.com/p/call-for-submissions-the-water-issu
 e?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://tractorbeamearth.substack.com/p/call-for-submissions-the-water-
 issue?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Online publication (remote submissions\; geographic eligibility no
 t stated)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:berggruen-prize-essay-competition-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T222051Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260817
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260818
SUMMARY:Deadline: Berggruen Prize Essay Competition 2026: A New Axial Age?
DESCRIPTION:Annual essay competition from the Berggruen Institute\, a compa
 nion to the Berggruen Prize for Philosophy & Culture\, seeking new thinkin
 g and cross-cultural perspectives on fundamental philosophical questions o
 f significance for contemporary life and the future. It looks for essays i
 n the tradition of Rousseau\, Montaigne\, and Emerson (models cited includ
 e Frankfurt\, Nagel\, Sontag\, Fukuyama\, and recent Noema pieces)\, not p
 eer-reviewed academic work: novel ideas\, clearly argued in compelling way
 s for intellectually serious readers. THEME for 2026: 'A New Axial Age?' R
 eferencing Karl Jaspers's Axial Age (c. 800-200 BCE)\, the prompt asks whe
 ther civilisation is again at a 'hinge of history' undergoing a pivotal tr
 ansformation of consciousness driven by rapid technological change and its
  social\, economic\, and political consequences. Suggested (non-exhaustive
 ) angles: the foundational ideas and institutions underpinning present and
  future societies\; the philosophical implications of the current era\; th
 e future of human evolution and life amid the ecological crisis\; shared c
 onceptual foundations for a common future\; global transformation driven b
 y technology and the rise of AI\; the end of the global order and its rami
 fications\; and the future of life amid biotechnology and synthetic life. 
 Entrants may choose their own topic\; for 2026 creative fiction is accepte
 d. ELIGIBILITY: open to the public\; not for academic papers and not gover
 ned by academic disciplinary standards or affiliations. Length up to 10\,0
 00 words in English or 15\,000 characters in Chinese (excluding diagrams\,
  tables\, endnotes\, bibliography\, or authorship declaration). One submis
 sion per year per entrant\, in either English or Chinese\; co-authorship a
 llowed. Submissions must be original and previously unpublished. AI USAGE:
  generative AI must not be used to create content or substitute original t
 hought\, and entrants must engage primary sources rather than AI summaries
 \; AI is permitted only for proofreading/grammar\, as quoted supporting ma
 terial when AI itself is the subject of the research\, or for translating 
 an essay into English or Chinese\, and all AI use (including AI-assisted t
 ranslation) must be disclosed on the front page or the entry is disqualifi
 ed. JUDGING: originality\, clarity\, and style. Essays are due by 23:59 PT
  on 17 August 2026\; PDF only. Chinese-language submissions use a separate
  page. Inquiries to essay@berggruen.org.\n\nOrganisation: Berggruen Instit
 ute\nLocation: Remote/online\; open to the public worldwide (English and C
 hinese submissions)\nAward: USD 50\,000 for the winning English essay and 
 USD 50\,000 for the winning Chinese essay. If there is more than one winne
 r in a language category\, the prize is split evenly. Winning essays are p
 ublished through BI Press or Noema. No entry fee.\nDuration: One-off compe
 tition\; submissions open 17 March 2026 and close 17 August 2026\n\nMore: 
 https://berggruen.org/eu/essay-competition-open?utm_source=artificialnouve
 augrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://berggruen.org/eu/essay-competition-open?utm_source=artificialno
 uveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote/online\; open to the public worldwide (English and Chinese 
 submissions)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:queen-mary-wasafiri-new-writing-prize-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T222051Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260630
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260701
SUMMARY:Deadline: Queen Mary Wasafiri New Writing Prize 2026
DESCRIPTION:Annual international writing prize from Wasafiri and Queen Mary
  University of London\, with three categories: Poetry\, Fiction\, and Life
  Writing (creative nonfiction). The prize is specifically for writers who 
 have NOT yet published a book-length work\, making it strongly debut-frien
 dly. The Life Writing category suits a self-contained creative-nonfiction 
 piece (for example an extract or essay drawn from a longer project). ELIGI
 BILITY: open to anyone who has not published a complete book\; no restrict
 ions on age\, gender\, nationality or background. Deadline 11:59pm BST\, 3
 0 June 2026. NOTE: entry fee applies (GBP 12/16\, or a subsidised GBP 6).\
 n\nOrganisation: Wasafiri and Queen Mary University of London\nLocation: O
 nline (UK-based\, international entry)\nAward: GBP 1\,000 cash plus print 
 publication for the winner of each category. Entry fee GBP 12 (single) / G
 BP 16 (double)\; a subsidised GBP 6 fee is available with no proof require
 d.\nDuration: Annual prize\n\nMore: https://www.wasafiri.org/writing-prize
 s/queen-mary-wasafiri-new-writing-prize/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrant
 desk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.wasafiri.org/writing-prizes/queen-mary-wasafiri-new-writing
 -prize/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Online (UK-based\, international entry)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:oif-artist-mobility-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260827T222051Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: OIF: Support for Artist Mobility and Circulation of Cultu
 ral Goods 2026 (Francophonie)
DESCRIPTION:The OIF Support for Artist Mobility and Circulation of Cultural
  Goods programme provides grants of up to EUR 5\,000 for mobility (travel\
 , accommodation\, visa fees\, logistics) and up to EUR 2\,500 for circulat
 ion of cultural goods (excess baggage\, freight\, customs) within the Fran
 cophone zone\; both can be combined. ELIGIBILITY: professional artists and
  established artistic collectives with a demonstrated connection to the Fr
 ancophone space (by nationality\, working language\, or artistic collabora
 tions)\, residing in an OIF member state\, with minimum 2 years of experie
 nce in the cultural or artistic sector\; must directly manage the project 
 (not act as intermediaries)\; one application per individual per call. ELI
 GIBLE DISCIPLINES: live performance (music\, theatre\, dance\, comedy\, st
 orytelling)\, cinema/audiovisual\, literature/publishing\, and visual arts
 . PRIORITY given to projects promoting gender equality\, women artists\, c
 reators under 35\, and South-South mobility. Projects must take place betw
 een 25 March and 30 November 2026. Multiple selection sessions throughout 
 the year\; call open until 31 August 2026. Information webinars: 11 March\
 , 13 May\, and 8 July 2026 at 3 PM Paris time. Contact: culture.dlc@franco
 phonie.org. Apply at https://appelsaprojets.francophonie.org/fr/applicatio
 ns/soutien-a-la-circulation-des-biens-et-mobilite-des-artistes.\n\nOrganis
 ation: Organisation internationale de la Francophonie (OIF)\nLocation: Fra
 ncophone zone (OIF member states globally)\nAward: Up to EUR 5\,000 for ar
 tist mobility (travel\, accommodation\, visas\, logistics). Up to EUR 2\,5
 00 for circulation of cultural goods (excess baggage\, freight\, customs).
  Both can be combined. No application fee.\nDuration: Residencies or tours
  of 5 days to 3 months. Projects must take place between 25 March and 30 N
 ovember 2026.\n\nMore: https://appelsaprojets.francophonie.org/fr/applicat
 ions/soutien-a-la-circulation-des-biens-et-mobilite-des-artistes?utm_sourc
 e=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
